What was done to the vendored tree, precisely
---------------------------------------------

It was MOVED. The tree used to sit at Heimdall/ in the repository root, where it
was read-only reference material. It now sits at native/vendor/Heimdall/ and is
COMPILED INTO the application: its C++ is built into the native library that
OdinCore calls, so the Samsung download-mode protocol runs as Heimdall's own
code rather than as a reimplementation of it.

Apart from the move, exactly four files are edited. Every edit is marked in
place with a comment beginning "EnneaOdin:".

  heimdall/source/BridgeManager.cpp
      NINE separate kinds of change.

      (a) Hooks, which are inert without a hook context. One include of
      native/src/OdinHooks.h, and nine call sites that use it: a cancellation
      check in each of the two wait-for-device poll loops (DetectDevice and
      FindDeviceInterface, once per one-second poll), one in the PIT receive
      loop (ReceivePitFile, once per PIT part), one in the file transfer loop
      (SendFile, once per file part), one progress report in that same loop,
      a cancellation check and a cancellable sleep in EACH of the two
      bulk-transfer retry loops (SendBulkTransfer and ReceiveBulkTransfer),
      and the session-banner pause described in (c).

      With no hook context installed - the case for every caller except the
      EnneaOdin bridge - Cancelled() is false, Progress() does nothing,
      Attached() is false and Nap() sleeps the whole interval and returns
      true, so every one of these is a no-op and the file behaves as upstream
      does.

      The two retry-loop checks are worth stating on their own even though
      they are inert for a terminal caller. Upstream's retry loop is five
      attempts with 250, 500, 750, 1000 and 1250 ms between them and a
      kDefaultTimeoutSend of 3000 ms each, which is 21,750 ms per packet
      during which a Stop button does nothing at all. The check bounds that to
      one libusb timeout plus a 50 ms slice; the in-flight transfer itself
      cannot be interrupted, so that timeout is the floor.

      (b) THREE UNCONDITIONAL FIXES in SendFile, which DO change behaviour for
      every caller. They are stated plainly here rather than hidden behind (a):

        - The retry path now seeks back to the offset the failed part was read
          from before rebuilding the retry packet. Upstream rebuilt it from the
          already-advanced file position, so a retry sent THE NEXT PART'S BYTES
          as the retry of the failed one. The part-index check did not catch
          it in the case the retry exists for: if the device had genuinely
          missed part N it accepted the following chunk as part N and answered
          with index N, the check passed, part N was never written, and the
          image landed one packet short with nothing reported. This is a
          silent-data-corruption fix, which is why it is unconditional.
        - Both sends now pass the file size SendFile already computed, instead
          of each packet seeking to the end of the file and back to learn it.
          For a multi-gigabyte image that removed thousands of seek pairs, each
          of which also discarded stdio's read buffer.
        - A short read from disk is now a hard failure. Upstream ignored
          fread's return value over a zero-filled buffer, so an I/O error
          shipped a packet of zeros to the device and the flash continued.

      (c) THE HANDSHAKE SENDS ONCE AND READS PATIENTLY. InitialiseProtocol
      sends with retry OFF and reads the reply with ReceiveBulkTransfer at
      3000 ms with retries, where upstream retried the send six times and then
      allowed the reply one bare 1000 ms libusb_bulk_transfer. It clears both
      endpoints first and names the libusb error behind every failure.

        MEASURED on an SM-A166U under macOS. macOS binds AppleUSBACMData to
        the CDC data interface and libusb cannot detach it without the
        com.apple.vm.device-access entitlement or root ("failed to auto-detach
        the kernel driver for this device" in the verbose log), so the claim
        succeeds while the pipes still belong to the kernel driver and the
        send times out. Those six send retries cost about ten seconds and
        never once helped; on the one attempt that got through, the send
        worked and the reply did not arrive inside upstream's single second.

        A RETRY LOOP HERE WAS TRIED AND REMOVED. Resetting the device between
        attempts cannot work from inside this function: libusb_reset_device
        re-enumerates the handset and invalidates the handle, so the later
        attempts ran against a dead one - "no connection to an IOService",
        then LIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE. Retrying therefore belongs one level up,
        where the device can be reset and then opened again from scratch, and
        that is where it now lives (OdinSession.OpenAndBeginAsync).

      (d) A SECOND TRANSPORT, CHOSEN BEFORE libusb OPENS THE DEVICE.
      SendBulkTransfer and ReceiveBulkTransfer, the two functions every byte of
      the protocol passes through, branch on which transport the session chose.
      TrySerialTransport runs FIRST, before FindDeviceInterface; ChooseTransport
      is the libusb-side fallback and runs after the claim.

        THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT. A libusb claim leaves the kernel driver's
        own pipes broken, and releasing the interface does not repair them, so
        the serial node stops carrying traffic as well until the device
        re-enumerates. An earlier version chose the transport AFTER claiming,
        which meant it had already broken the only path that works before it
        tried it. With the order corrected the handshake lands, the session
        opens, the PIT reads and the flash begins writing.

        WHY. On macOS, Samsung's download mode is a CDC-ACM gadget and the
        system binds AppleUSBACMData to its data interface.
        libusb_detach_kernel_driver returns LIBUSB_ERROR_ACCESS for BOTH
        interfaces and the backend says why: "USB device capture requires
        either an entitlement (com.apple.vm.device-access) or root
        privilege". libusb_claim_interface then SUCCEEDS anyway while
        libusb_kernel_driver_active still reports 1, so the pipes were never
        handed over and every bulk transfer returns LIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT.
        Measured on an SM-A166U under macOS 26, as root and as an ordinary
        user, with and without an explicit detach, with and without
        libusb_reset_device, and on both interfaces.

        ROOT IS NOT ENOUGH, which was measured rather than assumed: as root,
        with libusb_set_auto_detach_kernel_driver set, on a single handle with
        nothing closed in between, libusb_claim_interface returns success while
        libusb_kernel_driver_active still reports 1 and every bulk transfer
        times out. Capture needs the com.apple.vm.device-access entitlement,
        which Apple grants through a provisioning profile.

        WHAT INSTEAD. The kernel driver publishes the same two bulk endpoints
        as a serial node. Reaching the device through that node needs no
        entitlement and no root, and the handshake that times out on the bulk
        endpoints returns LOKE on the FIRST attempt there. The transport lives
        in src/OdinSerialPort.cpp, which is EnneaOdin's own code and not
        derived from Heimdall; this entry covers only the two-line branch at
        the top of each transfer function, the chooser, and the member they
        read.

        WHEN. Only when a kernel driver still holds the interface after a
        successful claim. On Linux, on Windows, and on macOS for a device no
        driver matched, that test is false and libusb is used exactly as
        before, byte for byte.

        ZERO-LENGTH TRANSFERS have no equivalent on a byte stream. Upstream
        already treats a failed one as "continue anyway", so on this transport
        they are reported as done without touching the port.

      (e) THE HANDSHAKE LATCH, AND THE ONE WAY OUT OF IT.

      InitialiseProtocol reads four bytes on the serial transport where
      upstream reads seven, and when nothing answers it sends a re-arm packet
      and tries once more. SendRearmPacket is added.

        FOUR BYTES, NOT SEVEN. Odin 3.14.1 writes "ODIN" and reads exactly 4
        before comparing with "LOKE" (sub_434D50). Upstream reads 7 into the
        buffer and checks that 4 arrived, which works on a bulk endpoint
        because a short packet ends the transfer. A byte stream has no packet
        boundaries, so asking for 7 waits out a timeout for three bytes that
        are never sent.

        THE LATCH. Reversed from the handset's own bootloader, s-lk.img for an
        SM-A166U, in usbd3_rdx_process:

            if (latch == 0) {
                if (!strncmp(rx, "ODIN", 4)) { latch = 1; reply = "LOKE"; }
                ...
            } else {
                ... accumulate 1024 bytes ...
                if (packet[0] == 103 && packet[1] == 4) {
                    offset = 0;
                    latch = 0;
                }
            }

        The handset answers ODIN exactly ONCE per entry into download mode.
        Every later handshake is received and ignored, which is why a second
        attempt looks identical to a dead transport: the bytes go out, the
        device takes them, nothing comes back. Black-box testing had already
        shown the behaviour; the listing shows the mechanism and the escape.

        The escape is a 1024-byte control packet whose first two little-endian
        words are 103 and 4. 103 is ControlPacket::kControlTypeEndSession. 4
        is a request Heimdall does not define - EndSessionPacket.h has only
        kRequestEndSession (0) and kRequestRebootDevice (1). It is sent only
        after a handshake went unanswered, because the branch that reads it is
        the one the latch being SET selects.

      (f) A DEVICE-INFORMATION REQUEST, RequestDeviceInfo, which is new and
      not an adaptation of anything upstream has.

        REVERSED FROM THE HANDSET'S OWN BOOTLOADER, s-lk.img for an SM-A166U.
        The first command of a download-mode session is matched against a FLAT
        chain, not just "ODIN" (the chain is at 0xF4810530, its string table at
        0xF49D0E70): ODIN, FPGM, PROMPT, DVIF, AT+ENGMODES,
        AT+ENGMODES=1,3,1, @#AuToTEst@#, @#AuToTEstRst@#.

        DVIF answers with up to 256 bytes:

          @#MODEL=..;UN=..;CAPA=..;VENDOR=..;FWVER=..;PRODUCT=..;PROV=..;
            SALES=..;VER=..;DID=..;TMU_TEMP=..;NAD_TEMP=..;@#

        and its branch does NOT set the latch that "ODIN" sets, so asking
        costs nothing: the handset still answers one ODIN afterwards.

        The model, firmware version, sales code and capacity are therefore
        available over the download-mode protocol. EnneaOdin used to state the
        opposite, in DeviceInformation.ScopeNote, and that was wrong.

      (g) DIAGNOSTICS, which change what is PRINTED and nothing else.

        - InitialiseProtocol now names the libusb error behind "Failed to send
          handshake!" and behind a failed handshake response, instead of
          printing the sentence alone. A stalled pipe, a vanished device and a
          timeout are three different problems with three different answers,
          and upstream printed the same line for all of them. A member,
          lastTransferResult, carries the code out of the transfer helpers.
        - InitialiseProtocol clears both bulk endpoints before the first
          transfer. An endpoint left halted by a previous attempt rejects
          everything until it is cleared; this is the session's first
          transfer, so there is nothing to lose, and a failure is reported
          rather than fatal.
        - SetupDeviceInterface no longer returns early when the alternate
          setting is 0. Upstream's comment was "there should be no need to set
          alt_setting(?)" - a question mark, not a finding. It is now always
          attempted; at 0 a failure is reported and ignored, so a device that
          upstream's guess was right about is unaffected.

      (h) KERNEL DRIVER DETACH, WHICH UPSTREAM DOES ONLY ON LINUX.

        Upstream wraps the whole detach-and-reclaim fallback, the
        detachedDriver member that records it, and the re-attach on release in
        "#ifdef OS_LINUX". A kernel driver sitting on the interface is not a
        Linux-only situation: on macOS, Samsung's download mode is a CDC-ACM
        gadget that AppleUSBACMData binds to on sight. So:

        - FindDeviceInterface calls libusb_set_auto_detach_kernel_driver before
          the claim, and reports what it returned.
        - The detach-then-reclaim fallback in ClaimDeviceInterface, and the
          re-attach in ReleaseDeviceInterface, are no longer Linux only. A
          platform that cannot detach returns an error and the second claim
          fails exactly as the first did, so nothing is lost by trying.
        - InitialiseProtocol resets the device once and retries the handshake
          when the first attempt fails. Upstream resets only when IsUbuntu()
          reads a matching /etc/issue; Heimdall PR 478 and the Arch AUR patch
          both reset unconditionally for this failure. It is done as a retry,
          so a device that never needed it is never reset, and nothing has
          been written to the handset at that point.

        MEASURED, so that the next reader does not have to repeat it: on this
        machine the detach is supported and does take effect, and it did NOT
        fix a handset whose bulk endpoints answer nothing. It is kept because
        it is correct and because claiming without it is what produced a
        silent timeout, not because it was the cure for that handset.

      (i) THE FILE PART SIZE IS EIGHT TIMES UPSTREAM'S, 8 MiB instead of
      1 MiB, with four parts a sequence instead of thirty so a sequence stays
      at 32 MiB rather than growing to 240.

        The handset's own limits, from its bootloader (s-lk.img,
        process_packet): the part size arrives in control packet {100, 5} and
        is rejected only when a byte above bit 23 is set, so anything under
        16 MiB is accepted; the per-sequence byte count arrives in {102, 2}
        and is rejected at or above 152 MiB. Both are CHECKED by the handset
        and answered with a failure code that Heimdall propagates, so an
        over-large value fails loudly rather than writing something wrong.

        8 MiB x 4 = 32 MiB a sequence, which is upstream's 30 MiB with an
        eighth of the round trips: a 6.7 GiB super.img goes from roughly 6700
        part exchanges to roughly 840.

        COUPLED TO THE MANAGED SIDE. A failed part is retried by re-reading
        it, so RewindStream.DefaultWindowBytes has to be at least one part; it
        was 4 MiB for a 1 MiB part and is now 32 MiB. The windowtest suite
        checks the two numbers against each other, and against both handset
        limits, so neither can drift without a failing test.

      (j) THE PERCENTAGE IS NOT PRINTED when a host has a progress callback
      installed. Upstream draws it in place with backspaces, which a log pane
      cannot render: it arrives as one line per step, so a single partition
      contributes up to a hundred lines between the two that matter. The byte
      counts the callback already receives carry the same information.

      (k) THE HANDSHAKE DRAINS THE PORT, THE DATA PATH DOES NOT. On the
      serial transport InitialiseProtocol waits for the output queue before it
      listens for LOKE, because that one exchange is strictly request then
      response. SerialPort::Write deliberately does not drain: emptying the
      queue after every write lets the pipe go idle between parts, once per
      8 MiB for a nine gigabyte flash.

        Odin 3.14.1 does the same thing. Its write (sub_438300) is WriteFile
        on an overlapped handle followed by GetOverlappedResult with
        bWait TRUE, and there is no FlushFileBuffers and no wait for the queue
        anywhere in it. It also does not pipeline: one buffer at a time, each
        awaited, exactly as this does.

      (l) TWO SMALL CHANGES IN BeginSession.

        - The three-second Sleep after "Some devices may take up to 2 minutes
          to respond" now runs only when no hook context is installed. The
          upstream comment says it is there to "Give the user time to read the
          message", which is right for a terminal and is three seconds of dead
          time for a graphical host that already printed the same sentence in
          its log. A terminal build is unchanged, because Attached() is false
          for it.
        - The begin-session response's packet size is now also stored on the
          BridgeManager, in a member added for it. Upstream reads it into a
          local, branches on whether it is zero and discards it; zero is the
          device saying it will not accept a packet-size change, which is
          worth reporting. Nothing branches on the stored copy.

  heimdall/source/SendFilePartPacket.h
      The other half of the three fixes above. A new three-argument
      constructor that takes the size the caller already knows; corrected
      remaining-bytes arithmetic (upstream computed min(fileSize, size) where
      the comment and the intent both said min(size, fileSize - position));
      and three read-only accessors - ShortRead, GetBytesRead and
      GetReadOffset - with the three private members behind them. The original
      two-argument FILE * constructor is kept and still measures the size
      itself, so any caller using the old signature behaves as before rather
      than silently sending zeros. All three constructors initialise all three
      members.

  heimdall/source/BridgeManager.h
      Four read-only accessors added next to the existing GetUsbLogLevel and
      IsVerbose: GetFileTransferPacketSize, GetFileTransferSequenceMaxLength,
      GetFileTransferSequenceTimeout and GetDeviceDefaultPacketSize. The first
      three read members that are private and that BeginSession settles, so a
      caller that puts the numbers on screen had no way to read them and would
      have had to guess which set was in force.

      detachedDriver is no longer declared under "#ifdef OS_LINUX", matching
      the detach change in BridgeManager.cpp (d).

      A SerialPort member and the flag that selects it are added, with two
      read-only accessors for them, for the transport described in
      BridgeManager.cpp (d). ChooseTransport is declared.

      TWO MEMBERS ARE ADDED. deviceDefaultPacketSize, written once in
      BeginSession from the begin-session response and once in Initialise to
      zero it. It is read by its accessor and by nothing else, so no protocol
      decision depends on it. And lastTransferResult, mutable because the
      transfer helpers are const, written by every bulk transfer and read only
      to name an error in a message. Neither changes a protocol decision. The
      first version of this entry said "no member is added", which was true of
      that version and is not true of this one.

  libpit/source/libpit.h
      Two one-word corrections, in PitEntry::SetFlashFilename and
      PitEntry::SetFotaFilename. Both guarded their strcpy with
      strlen(partitionName) - a different field - instead of the argument they
      were about to copy. Since the partitionName member is always terminated
      inside 32 bytes, the guard was always true and the strcpy had no length
      check at all, so a PIT whose 32-byte flash-filename or FOTA-filename
      field carried no terminator - the format does not require one - copied
      past the end of a 32-byte member. A PIT read from a device or chosen by
      the user reaches that code, which is why it is fixed here rather than
      worked around. Reported in this NOTICE because it is a behavioural change
      to vendored code, not only a safety one.

Nothing else is modified, reformatted or regenerated, including LICENSE and
heimdall/60-heimdall.rules. heimdall-frontend/ (the Qt front end) and
heimdall/source/main.cpp are present and are deliberately not built: the front
end is not needed, and main.cpp owns a process entry point that belongs to the
host application.

That claim is mechanically checkable against the pre-move history:

    for old in $(git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD Heimdall/); do
        new="native/vendor/${old}"
        git show "HEAD:$old" | cmp -s - "$new" || echo "CHANGED $old"
    done

On the two licences: Heimdall's MIT terms permit relicensing a derivative work
under the GPL, provided the MIT copyright notice and permission notice are
retained, which they are. They also permit modifying and compiling the code into
a larger work, which is what this project does. EnneaOdin's choice of GPL-3.0 is
a free choice by this project and not an obligation inherited from Heimdall.

EnneaOdin does not require, bundle, call or start any heimdall binary, and never
starts a child process to talk to a device. The protocol is in-process.

